IMPORT prelude

-- Estate Duty Act 1929 (Singapore) -- who the Act still reaches (s 2A), what property is within it (s 2 "property", s 11(2)), gifts
-- deemed to pass on death (s 7(1)(c)), the house, other-property and CPF reliefs for deaths from 1 April 1984 (s 14(3)-(8)), the
-- rates (s 5(d), (e), Fourth and Fifth Schedules), the funeral allowance (s 27(5)), the six-month return (s 35), interest and the
-- late-payment penalty (s 36(1)-(3), Sixth Schedule), the $50,000 share exception (s 43(5)), intermeddling (s 44), the small-sum
-- waiver (s 50) and false statements (s 54).
--
-- Edition: 2020 Revised Edition, informal consolidation, as deposited at ../../registers/source-bundle/EDA1929.txt, with amendments to
-- Act 5 of 2025 (in force 9 March 2025) shown. The Sixth Schedule table is laid out in interleaved columns in the .txt deposit; the
-- periods and rates were matched by their item numbers (1, 2, 3) and by order of appearance.
--
-- WHY THIS ACT. Cited by 13 of the 527 Singapore Acts deposited in this repository. SCOPED to the decisions an executor or a
-- practitioner closing an old estate meets. Not encoded: the deemed-passing rules other than gifts (s 7(1)(a), (b), (d)-(g), s 8),
-- companies (Part 3), aggregation, valuation and interests in expectancy (ss 23-26), foreign duty credits (s 28), liability and
-- apportionment (ss 29-34), the Commissioner's powers, certificates and postponement (ss 37-42), appeals (s 47), informers (s 54A),
-- deaths before 1 April 1984 (First to Third Schedules and the remission schedules), and s 14(2) and (9) for 1982-1984.
--
-- DATES are written as numbers of the form YYYYMMDD (20050101 is 1 January 2005); that is an encoding convention, not the Act's.
-- PERIODS (months since death, months unpaid) are whole complete months supplied by the user.
--
-- NAIVE ENCODING. Straight from the deposited text with the writing-l4-rules skill. No pipeline, no coverage table,
-- no independent test pass, no human gate. See NOTES.md.

§ `Estate Duty Act 1929 -- estates`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the lesser of` MEANS IF `a` AT MOST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

GIVEN `a` IS A NUMBER, `b` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the greater of` MEANS IF `a` AT LEAST `b` THEN `a` ELSE `b`

§§ `2A and 5 -- which deaths, which rates`

-- s 2A: "This Act shall apply only in relation to persons dying before 15 February 2008." [13/2008]
-- s 5: estate duty is levied on "the principal value ... of all property settled or not settled which passes on the death" at the
-- First Schedule rates (deaths 1 April 1974 to before 1 April 1977), Second (to before 1 April 1979), Third (to before 1 April 1984),
-- Fourth (to before 28 February 1996) and Fifth (28 February 1996 to before 15 February 2008).
-- s 5 sets no rate for a death before 1 April 1974: the Act still applies to such a death (s 2A) but its rates are not in s 5.
DECLARE Rates IS ONE OF
    `no rates set out in section 5`
    `the First Schedule`
    `the Second Schedule`
    `the Third Schedule`
    `the Fourth Schedule`
    `the Fifth Schedule`
    `none -- the Act does not apply`

GIVEN `date of death` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the Act applies to the death` IF `date of death` LESS THAN 20080215

GIVEN `date of death` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A Rates
`the rates that apply` MEANS
    IF `date of death` AT LEAST 20080215 THEN `none -- the Act does not apply`
    ELSE
        IF `date of death` AT LEAST 19960228 THEN `the Fifth Schedule`
        ELSE
            IF `date of death` AT LEAST 19840401 THEN `the Fourth Schedule`
            ELSE
                IF `date of death` AT LEAST 19790401 THEN `the Third Schedule`
                ELSE
                    IF `date of death` AT LEAST 19770401 THEN `the Second Schedule`
                    ELSE
                        IF `date of death` AT LEAST 19740401 THEN `the First Schedule`
                        ELSE `no rates set out in section 5`

§§ `2 and 11(2) -- what property is within the Act`

-- s 2: "property" includes movable and immovable property "situate or being in Singapore" and, for a person "domiciled in Singapore",
-- "includes movable property wherever it may be". So immovable property outside Singapore is never within the definition.
-- s 11(2): duty "shall not be payable in respect of any movable property passing on the death of any person dying on or after
-- 1 January 2002 who at the time of his death was not domiciled in Singapore". (s 11(1)'s list of exempt deposits and bonds for
-- non-residents dying 1 April 1982 to before 1 January 2002 is not encoded.)
GIVEN `domiciled in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN, `movable` IS A BOOLEAN, `situate in Singapore` IS A BOOLEAN, `date of death` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the property is dutiable` IF
        `the Act applies to the death` `date of death`
    AND (`situate in Singapore` OR (`domiciled in Singapore` AND `movable`))
    AND NOT (`movable` AND (NOT `domiciled in Singapore`) AND `date of death` AT LEAST 20020101)

§§ `7(1)(c) -- lifetime gifts deemed to pass on death`

-- s 7(1)(c): property taken under a gift "which shall not have been bona fide made 5 years before his death", or under any gift
-- "whenever made" of which the donee did not assume possession and enjoyment immediately and "to the entire exclusion of the donor or
-- of any benefit to him". (i) For a gift "made for public or charitable purposes", "5 years" reads "12 months". (ii) Nothing in the
-- paragraph applies to gifts "in consideration of marriage", to the Government or an institution of a public character, gifts proved
-- to be "part of the normal expenditure of the deceased" and reasonable, or gifts which to any donee "do not exceed in the aggregate
-- $1,000".
-- INFERENCE: "made 5 years before his death" is read as made at least 60 complete months before the death.
GIVEN `months before death` IS A NUMBER, `for public or charitable purposes` IS A BOOLEAN, `donor kept a benefit` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `in consideration of marriage` IS A BOOLEAN, `to the Government or a public institution` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `proved normal expenditure` IS A BOOLEAN, `aggregate to this donee` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the gift is deemed to pass on death` IF
        (NOT `in consideration of marriage`)
    AND (NOT `to the Government or a public institution`)
    AND (NOT `proved normal expenditure`)
    AND `aggregate to this donee` GREATER THAN 1000
    AND (    `donor kept a benefit`
         OR  (`for public or charitable purposes` AND `months before death` LESS THAN 12)
         OR  ((NOT `for public or charitable purposes`) AND `months before death` LESS THAN 60))

§§ `14(3) to (8) -- the house, other-property and CPF reliefs`

-- s 14(4): for a death on or after 28 February 1996, duty is not payable "to the extent of" (a) "the amount prescribed of the
-- aggregate value of the deceased's interest in a dwelling house or dwelling houses, whether occupied by the deceased or not";
-- (b) "$600,000 of the aggregate value of all other property, including any interest in any dwelling house which does not qualify";
-- (c) "the excess over $600,000, if any," of the deceased's CPF and designated pension or provident fund balances.
-- s 14(3) is the same for deaths 1 April 1984 to before 28 February 1996, with $500,000 in (b) and (c).
-- s 14(8) "amount prescribed": $3 million (1 April 1984 to before 28 February 1996); $9 million (on or after 28 February 1996).
-- s 14(5): where the house value "exceeds the amount prescribed, the excess value shall not qualify for relief under" (b).
-- The relieved amount "shall not form part of the principal value of the estate chargeable".
-- INFERENCE: CPF balances up to the threshold are "other property" within (b); only the excess is relieved by (c). The values given
-- are taken as already net of debts and of the funeral allowance; the Act's order of deduction is not modelled. The CPF proviso on
-- contributions made on or after 1 April 1982 (relief only if deductible under the Income Tax Act) is not modelled: all CPF given is
-- treated as qualifying.
GIVEN `date of death` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the amount prescribed for a house` MEANS
    IF `date of death` AT LEAST 19960228 THEN 9000000
    ELSE
        IF `date of death` AT LEAST 19840401 THEN 3000000
        ELSE 0

GIVEN `date of death` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the other-property relief` MEANS
    IF `date of death` AT LEAST 19960228 THEN 600000
    ELSE
        IF `date of death` AT LEAST 19840401 THEN 500000
        ELSE 0

-- s 14(6): no house relief for a dwelling house "(a) used wholly or partly for the purposes of any trade, business, profession or
-- vocation" or "(b) passing on the death ... by virtue of section 7(1)(c)". s 14(7): for a death on or after 25 February 2000, (6)(a)
-- does not apply to a house used only PARTLY for business under the Home Office Scheme or the small business guidelines (URA or HDB).
-- s 14(8): "dwelling house" does not include one "used wholly or partly as a hostel or quarters".
GIVEN `used wholly for business` IS A BOOLEAN, `used partly for business` IS A BOOLEAN, `under the Home Office Scheme` IS A BOOLEAN,
      `passing as a gift under section 7(1)(c)` IS A BOOLEAN, `used as a hostel or quarters` IS A BOOLEAN, `date of death` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the house qualifies for the house relief` IF
        (NOT `used as a hostel or quarters`)
    AND (NOT `passing as a gift under section 7(1)(c)`)
    AND (NOT `used wholly for business`)
    AND (    (NOT `used partly for business`)
         OR  (`under the Home Office Scheme` AND `date of death` AT LEAST 20000225))

GIVEN `date of death` IS A NUMBER, `qualifying house value` IS A NUMBER, `other property value` IS A NUMBER, `CPF balance` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the section 14 relief` MEANS
        `the lesser of` `qualifying house value` (`the amount prescribed for a house` `date of death`)
    PLUS `the lesser of` (`other property value` PLUS `the lesser of` `CPF balance` (`the other-property relief` `date of death`))
                         (`the other-property relief` `date of death`)
    PLUS `the greater of` 0 (`CPF balance` MINUS `the other-property relief` `date of death`)

GIVEN `date of death` IS A NUMBER, `qualifying house value` IS A NUMBER, `other property value` IS A NUMBER, `CPF balance` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the chargeable principal value` MEANS
    `the greater of` 0
        (`qualifying house value` PLUS `other property value` PLUS `CPF balance`
         MINUS `the section 14 relief` `date of death` `qualifying house value` `other property value` `CPF balance`)

§§ `5(d), (e), Fourth and Fifth Schedules -- the duty`

-- Fourth Schedule (1 April 1984 to before 28 February 1996): "For every dollar of the first $10,000,000 -- 5%"; "exceeding
-- $10,000,000 -- 10%". Fifth Schedule (28 February 1996 to before 15 February 2008): the same with $12,000,000.
GIVEN `chargeable value` IS A NUMBER, `band` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`two-band duty` MEANS
        `the lesser of` `chargeable value` `band` TIMES 5 DIVIDED BY 100
    PLUS `the greater of` 0 (`chargeable value` MINUS `band`) TIMES 10 DIVIDED BY 100

GIVEN `date of death` IS A NUMBER, `chargeable value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the estate duty on the chargeable value` MEANS
    IF `date of death` AT LEAST 20080215 THEN 0
    ELSE
        IF `date of death` AT LEAST 19960228 THEN `two-band duty` `chargeable value` 12000000
        ELSE
            IF `date of death` AT LEAST 19840401 THEN `two-band duty` `chargeable value` 10000000
            ELSE 0

GIVEN `date of death` IS A NUMBER, `qualifying house value` IS A NUMBER, `other property value` IS A NUMBER, `CPF balance` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the estate duty` MEANS
    `the estate duty on the chargeable value` `date of death`
        (`the chargeable principal value` `date of death` `qualifying house value` `other property value` `CPF balance`)

§§ `27(5) -- the funeral allowance`

-- s 27(1): allowance for "reasonable funeral expenses not exceeding the amount specified in subsection (5)". (5): (a) dying before
-- 1 January 2005, "$1,000 or 5% of the ascertained value of the estate, whichever is the smaller"; (b) on or after, "$6,000".
GIVEN `date of death` IS A NUMBER, `funeral expenses` IS A NUMBER, `ascertained value` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the funeral allowance` MEANS
    IF `date of death` AT LEAST 20050101
    THEN `the lesser of` `funeral expenses` 6000
    ELSE `the lesser of` `funeral expenses` (`the lesser of` 1000 (`ascertained value` TIMES 5 DIVIDED BY 100))

§§ `35, 36 and Sixth Schedule -- the return, interest and penalty`

-- s 35(1): the executor must "within 6 months after the date of the death" deliver an estate duty return; (6) the Commissioner may
-- extend the period.
GIVEN `months since death` IS A NUMBER, `return delivered` IS A BOOLEAN, `period extended` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the executor is late with the return` IF
    (NOT `return delivered`) AND `months since death` AT LEAST 6 AND (NOT `period extended`)

-- Sixth Schedule, deaths on or after 1 January 2005: "1. For the first 6 months after the date of death -- 0%"; 2. from 6 months to
-- the earliest of a complete return, notice of assessment, full payment or "expiration of 18 months" -- "6% per year"; 3. from
-- 18 months -- "12% per year". (The Commissioner may reduce 2 and 3 for reasonable cause, to not less than the prime lending rate.)
-- Deaths 1 January 1965 to before 1 January 2005: 1. to 6 months -- 3% (reduced to zero if a complete return arrived within
-- 6 months); 2. 6 to 18 months -- 6% (or 3% for reasonable cause); 3. after 18 months -- 12% (or 6%).
-- s 36(1)(b): for deaths on or after 1 January 2005, interest stops at the earliest of a complete return, the notice of assessment
-- or payment.
GIVEN `died before 2005` IS A BOOLEAN, `months since death` IS A NUMBER, `complete return within 6 months` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the Sixth Schedule rate` MEANS
    IF `months since death` LESS THAN 6
    THEN
        IF `died before 2005` AND (NOT `complete return within 6 months`) THEN 3
        ELSE 0
    ELSE
        IF `months since death` LESS THAN 18 THEN 6
        ELSE 12

-- s 36(2), deaths on or after 1 January 2005: if duty is unpaid on the "relevant date", "(a) a penalty equal to 6% per year ... for
-- the first complete month"; "(b) an additional penalty of 1% per year ... for each subsequent complete month ... subject to a maximum
-- of 12% per year". (3) "relevant date" means "the date 30 days after the date of the notice of assessment, or the date 6 months
-- after the date of death ... whichever is the later".
GIVEN `complete months unpaid after the relevant date` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the late payment penalty rate` MEANS
    IF `complete months unpaid after the relevant date` LESS THAN 1 THEN 0
    ELSE `the lesser of` 12 (5 PLUS `complete months unpaid after the relevant date`)

-- Days are counted from the death. INFERENCE: "6 months after the date of death" is approximated by the user as a day count.
GIVEN `days from death to notice of assessment` IS A NUMBER, `days from death to six months` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the relevant date in days after death` MEANS
    `the greater of` (`days from death to notice of assessment` PLUS 30) `days from death to six months`

§§ `43(5) and 44 -- dealing with the estate`

-- s 43(1): no dealings with a deceased person's shares may be registered unless they are in the s 41 schedule or a certificate.
-- (5): this does not apply to shares in a Central Depository securities account, or in a CPF Investment Account, whose "aggregate
-- market value ... as at the date of his death, does not exceed $50,000". (3): failure without reasonable excuse -- a sum not
-- exceeding $500.
GIVEN `held with the Central Depository or CPF Investment Account` IS A BOOLEAN, `aggregate market value` IS A NUMBER,
      `in the schedule or certificate` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the share dealing may be registered` IF
        `in the schedule or certificate`
    OR  (`held with the Central Depository or CPF Investment Account` AND `aggregate market value` AT MOST 50000)

-- s 44(1): an accountable person who "wilfully fails" to deliver the s 35 return is liable to pay "double the amount of the estate
-- duty for which he is accountable". (2): except under s 43(5), a person who takes possession of or administers any part of a
-- dutiable estate "without obtaining probate or letters of administration ... within 6 months after the death" (or within 2 months
-- after the end of a will or administration dispute not ended within 4 months of the death) "shall forfeit ... $1,000 and shall
-- also be liable to pay ... double the amount of duty leviable".
-- INFERENCE: "double the amount of duty" is read as an amount of twice the duty; whether it is in addition to the single duty is not
-- said in s 44 and is not decided here. s 36(4) speaks of "double duty" replacing the single duty for interest purposes.
GIVEN `took possession or administered` IS A BOOLEAN, `obtained a grant in time` IS A BOOLEAN, `within the share exception` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the intermeddling penalty applies` IF
    `took possession or administered` AND (NOT `obtained a grant in time`) AND (NOT `within the share exception`)

GIVEN `duty leviable` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the intermeddling sums` MEANS 1000 PLUS 2 TIMES `duty leviable`

GIVEN `wilfully failed to deliver the return` IS A BOOLEAN, `duty accountable` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the wilful failure liability` MEANS IF `wilfully failed to deliver the return` THEN 2 TIMES `duty accountable` ELSE 0

§§ `50 and 54 -- small sums and false statements`

-- s 50: the Commissioner "may waive" duty or additional duty not exceeding $10, and interest not exceeding $5.
GIVEN `duty owed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the duty may be waived` IF `duty owed` AT MOST 10

GIVEN `interest owed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the interest may be waived` IF `interest owed` AT MOST 5

-- s 54: a person who "knowingly makes any false statement or false representation" to obtain an allowance, reduction, rebate or
-- repayment, or in any return, affidavit or document, is guilty of an offence: fine not exceeding $1,000 or imprisonment not exceeding
-- 2 years or both.
GIVEN `made a false statement` IS A BOOLEAN, `knew it was false` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `a false statement offence is committed` IF `made a false statement` AND `knew it was false`

GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum fine for a false statement` MEANS 1000

GIVETH A NUMBER
`the maximum prison term in months for a false statement` MEANS 24
